IAM

IAM - Identity and Access Management

	Process of controlling the access in Azure


Authentication: Confirms identity of a user, device, or service.


Authorization:  Determines what authenticated identities can access or perform.



Aspect			Authentication (AuthN)			Authorization (AuthZ)
Definition		Verifies identity			Grants/denies access rights
Question Answered	“Who are you?”				“What can you do?”
Timing	First step	Follows authentication
Techniques		Passwords, MFA, biometrics		RBAC, ABAC, policies, scopes
Output			Identity token/session	Access  	decision (allow/deny)
Failure Risk		Identity theft, weak passwords		Over-permission, privilege escalation


Microsoft Entra ID (Azure Active Directory)

	-- Users
	-- Applications
	-- Groups
	-- Devices

RBAC : Role Based Access Control - is an IAM authorization model where permissions are grouped into roles, and users are assigned roles instead of individual permissions. This simplifies access management, enforces least privilege, and scales well in large organizations.


Roles:

	Owner --> He can assign / delete / create --> Highest Privilege

	Contributor --> Can create, modify and delete but cannot assign roles

	Reader --> Can only view


Custom Roles:-  Admins / Owners will create the custom roles based on the requirement.


Azure App Service:
What it is: A fully managed platform for building, deploying, and scaling web apps, APIs, and mobile backends.

Supported stacks: .NET, Java, Node.js, Python, PHP, and custom containers.

Azure App Service Plan:
What it is: Defines the compute resources (CPU, memory, disk, scaling) that your App Service apps run on.



Aspect		App Service (Web App/API)		App Service Plan (Compute)
Purpose		Hosts your application			Provides compute resources
Focus		Code, runtime, features			Scaling, pricing, hardware
Dependency	Needs a plan to run			Can host multiple apps
Example		A Node.js API backend			Standard S1 plan with 2 cores, 3.5 GB RAM


